   accept the AGPL, and in research. It requires that if you make a modified version available to
   others over a network, or distribute the software (including as part of a larger work), you do
   so under the AGPL as well.

2. A commercial licence, described here, for the cases the AGPL does not suit. It is free of
   charge for small licensees and paid for larger ones, on the threshold below.

## The revenue threshold

The commercial licence costs nothing if, in the licensee's most recent fiscal year, its total
annual revenue - across the licensee and all of its affiliates - was **below USD 5,000,000** (five
million US dollars).

At or above USD 5,000,000 the licence is paid, and is granted by written agreement. The fee, the
scope and the term are agreed in writing; this file deliberately has no price list.

The figure is self-reported. There is no audit, no certificate and nothing to sign either way:
under the threshold, this file is the grant.

## When you need the commercial licence

You need it if either applies, and you are above the revenue threshold:

* **You offer swadesh, or a modified version of it, as a network service** and are unwilling or
  unable to publish the source of that service under the AGPL (AGPL section 13).
* **You ship swadesh inside a product distributed in binary or object form** - including an
  application, an appliance or an SDK - and do not wish to release that product under the AGPL.

## When you do not need any licence beyond the AGPL

* Running swadesh inside your own organisation to score your own data. This is free under the AGPL
  and needs no commercial licence, at any size.
* Using swadesh as a build- or evaluation-time tool whose output you own, where swadesh itself is
  not distributed to your users.
* Publishing your work under the AGPL, or an AGPL-compatible licence.

If you are unsure which side of a line you are on, ask before you ship.

## How to obtain it

Write to the copyright holder with a short description of the intended use:

